// For God so loved the world that he gave his only begotten Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life.
// Linguistic observations from the LLM merge pass — one JSON object per lemma
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5375","glossChirho":"נשׂא lift/carry","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Stem (binyan) is primary separator. Within Qal, idiomatic collocations create distinct senses: nasa panim (show partiality), nasa avon (forgive sin vs bear guilt — context-dependent), nasa rosh (take census). The forgive vs endure-consequence split is theologically significant."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7760a","glossChirho":"שׂים put/set","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic جَعَلَ vs وَضَعَ cleanly separates 'make into' (transformative) from 'put/place' (physical). The idiom שׂים לב (set heart = pay attention) is a distinct lexicalized expression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5674a","glossChirho":"עבר pass/cross","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Spanish cruzar vs pasar, Korean 건너 vs 지나가, Swahili kuvuka vs kupita all independently distinguish 'cross a boundary' from 'pass through'. Hifil proclaim sense (cause decree to pass through) is a specialized idiom."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1980","glossChirho":"הלך go/walk","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic salaka (follow a path) signals the metaphorical conduct sense vs dhahaba (physical going). Hitpael 'walk about' covers intimate companionship walking (Enoch walked with God, Gen 5:22)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8104","glossChirho":"שׁמר keep/guard","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal covers both physical guarding and obedient keeping of commandments in one sense — the multilingual glosses don't consistently separate these. Niphal 'beware/take heed' is reflexive self-guarding."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5493","glossChirho":"סור turn aside","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into physical removal vs moral purging (putting away idols/evil). Hindi utaarī and Korean beot-da (take off garments) mark the garment-removal sub-sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7725","glossChirho":"שׁוב return","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Arabic تُوبُوا (tawba = repentance) and Swahili tubuni distinguished the Qal repentance sense from physical return. Hifil 'return a word' = answer/reply is an important idiom (Spanish respondió, Swahili kujibu)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5975","glossChirho":"עמד stand","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic roots ث-ب-ت/ب-ق-ي/ص-م-د (endure) vs و-ق-ف (stop) separate the endure and stop sub-senses within Qal. Hindi ठहर/रुक (stop) vs बना रह/टिक (endure) confirmed."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4672","glossChirho":"מצא find","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَصَابَ (befall/strike) vs وَجَدَ (find) distinguishes 'calamity finds someone' from intentional finding. يَكْفِي (suffice) catches the rare 'be enough' sense. Niphal articulated participle הַנִּמְצְאִים is a lexicalized quasi-nominal 'those present'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3427","glossChirho":"ישׁב sit/dwell","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Spanish sentarse vs habitar vs quedarse, Arabic جلس vs سكن vs مكث, Korean 앉 vs 거주 vs 머물 all independently three-way-split sit/dwell/remain. Mega-cluster 104422 (539 occ) needed manual override from sit to dwell."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3045","glossChirho":"ידע know","sensesChirho":13,"notesChirho":"Spanish saber vs conocer and Arabic عَلِمَ vs عَرَفَ distinguish cognitive from relational knowledge. Sexual knowing (euphemism) confirmed across all languages. Pual participle 'acquaintance' is a lexicalized nominal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3381","glossChirho":"ירד go down","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into concrete bringing-down vs figurative sending-down (rain/judgment) — Arabic anuzilu vs ukhdi'u, Hindi utaarna vs barsaana. 'Those going down to the pit' (yorde bor) is a formulaic prophetic expression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5046","glossChirho":"נגד tell","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَخْبَرَ (inform) vs يُعْلِنُ (announce) separates tell/report from declare/proclaim. Swahili tangaza (announce publicly) vs ambia (tell) confirmed. Rare senses: denounce, confess, disclose each have distinctive multilingual evidence."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5221","glossChirho":"נכה strike","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Arabic qatala (kill) vs daraba (hit) and Swahili kuua vs kupiga separate lethal from non-lethal striking. Specialized sub-senses: clap hands, thrust/stab, drive in nail, defeat — each confirmed by distinct target-language verbs."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6965b","glossChirho":"קום rise","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Arabic yathbutu (be firm) vs yaqumu (rise) separates stand firm/endure from basic arising. Hifil splits 5 ways: raise person, erect object, establish covenant, fulfill promise, rouse from inactivity. Arabic jamuda (congeal) catches the rare physical 'stand still' sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8130","glossChirho":"שׂנא hate","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Sonnet collapsed to 2 senses; Opus found 6. Key: Qal participle 'hater/enemy' is a lexicalized nominal (Arabic عدو, Swahili adui) distinct from finite 'to hate'. Qal passive participle שְׂנוּאָה 'unloved wife' is adjectival/stative vs Nifal processual passive."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0142","glossChirho":"αἴρω lift/take","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Sonnet found 3; Opus found 8. Caught nautical 'weigh anchor' (Acts 27), hostile 'Away with him!' (John 19:15), and idiomatic 'keep in suspense' (John 10:24 τὴν ψυχὴν αἴρω). Korean 들어-올리다 (lift up) vs 가져가다 (take away) was diagnostic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7069","glossChirho":"קנה acquire/buy","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Spanish comprar vs adquirir vs creador separates buy/purchase from acquire/obtain from create/possess. Gen 14:19 Creator/Possessor of heaven: Arabic خالق, Swahili Muumba. Caught qana'/qanah conflation in Deut 32:21 (provoke to jealousy)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2076","glossChirho":"זבח sacrifice","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Swahili kuchinja (slaughter animal) vs kutoa dhabihu (offer sacrifice) was the cleanest discriminator for the slaughter-vs-sacrifice split. Matches HALOT Qal §1 vs §2-5. Could be refined further with syntactic frames (deity recipients, offering objects)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2428","glossChirho":"חיל strength/army","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides 5 distinct lexemes mapping to 5 senses: جيش (army), بأس (valor), ثروة (wealth), قوة (strength), فضيلة (virtue). Korean and Swahili independently confirmed all 5 splits. Best example of multilingual evidence resolving polysemy."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2891","glossChirho":"טהר be clean","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Critical Piel split: declarative 'pronounce clean' (priest's speech act, Lev 13-14) vs factitive 'make clean/purify' (actual removal of impurity). This is a real lexicographic distinction within the same stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0929","glossChirho":"בהמה beast/cattle","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Korean 짐승 (wild/generic beast) vs 가축 (domesticated livestock), Spanish bestia vs ganado, Swahili mnyama vs mifugo. Arabic بَهِيمَة covers both — less discriminating here."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G3860","glossChirho":"παραδίδωμι hand over","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Betray (Judas) vs hand over to custody vs give over/surrender vs entrust vs transmit tradition vs yield fruit. 'Transmit tradition' (1 Cor 11:23) is theologically crucial and distinct from betrayal despite same Greek verb. Hindi पकड़वाना (betray) vs सौंपना (hand over) was diagnostic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1431","glossChirho":"גדל be great/grow","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Piel splits: 'raise children' (Arabic رَبَّى, Korean 양육하다, Swahili -lea) vs 'magnify/extol' (Arabic عَظَّمَ, Korean 높이다, Swahili -tukuza) — different semantic domains within same stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3824","glossChirho":"לבב heart","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"52 of 54 clusters are one sense — glosses entirely uniform. Swahili katikati ya (middle of) vs moyo (heart) caught the rare spatial 'center/midst' sense. One adjectival 'whole/sound' cluster may be a tagging error."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8451","glossChirho":"תורה Torah/law","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Korean 율법 (law) vs 가르침 (teaching) and Swahili sheria vs mafundisho cleanly separate divine Torah from human instruction. Plural תּוֹרֹת morphologically and semantically distinct as individual regulations."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5650","glossChirho":"עבד servant","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Swahili mtumishi (free servant) vs mtumwa (slave/owned person) was the strongest signal for servant/slave distinction. 'Servant of God' honorific (Moses, David) identified by 1cs possessive + comparative preposition pattern."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8179","glossChirho":"שׁער gate","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Deuteronomic 'your gates' = 'your towns' metonymy identified by 2nd-person possessive suffix concentration on plural form. All other inflections are one sense — physical gate/entrance."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4725","glossChirho":"מקום place","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"51 of 52 clusters are one sense. Swahili nafasi (space/room) vs mahali (place) caught the single spatial-extent sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8210","glossChirho":"שׁפך pour out","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic سَكَبَ (pour liquid) vs سَفَكَ (shed blood) is a lexical split in Arabic mapping to a real Hebrew polysemy. Korean 쌓다 (pile up) caught the siege-ramp sense within the metaphorical group."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2181","glossChirho":"זנה commit harlotry","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Literal sexual harlotry and metaphorical spiritual unfaithfulness (prophetic idiom) were merged in Qal — flagged for refinement. Hifil causative confirmed by Spanish hizo fornicar, Hindi व्यभिचार कराया, Swahili akawafanya wazini."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6031b","glossChirho":"ענה afflict/humble","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Piel splits: afflict/oppress vs humble/bring low vs violate/ravish (sexual, Deut 22). Hitpael self-affliction = fasting. Caught Psalm 88 superscription debate: Arabic لِلْإِنْشَادِ and Korean 노래하기 위하여 support 'to sing/chant' (possible root ענה III)."}
